{% extends 'base.html.twig' %}

{% block title %}Choose a new password{% endblock %}

{% block body %}
    <h1>Choose a new password</h1>

    {# THE TOKEN APPEARS NOWHERE IN THIS FILE, AND THAT IS THE POINT OF THE WHOLE REDIRECT (AC-14,
       ADR-0011 decision 8). Not in a hidden input, not in the form's action, not in a link, not in a
       data attribute, not in a comment. It lives in the session; `PasswordResetController::reset()`
       reads it there and the browser never sees it again after the single redirect hop that got the
       visitor here.

       This is the page a person sits on and types into, which is exactly why the token must not be in
       its URL: browser history, cloud-synced history, a shoulder-surfable address bar, any outbound
       `Referer`, and whatever gets pasted into a support chat when they ask a friend for help. If a
       later change ever needs "the token" in this template, the change is wrong — `NewPasswordFormType`
       has no field to bind it to, and `allow_extra_fields: false` will reject one that is added by
       hand.

       It is also address-free (AC-36): there is nothing here that names the account being recovered,
       because nothing here needs to and a page that named it would be a page worth opening a stolen
       link for. #}

    <p>Pick something you do not use anywhere else. It must be at least 12 characters.</p>

    {# `form_start`/`form_end` render `new_password_form[_token]` — the CSRF token, which is a
       different thing from the reset token and the only token that belongs in this page's HTML. #}
    {{ form_start(newPasswordForm) }}
        {# Root-level errors: an invalid CSRF token and the `allow_extra_fields: false` rejection land
           on the form itself rather than on any field, and neither `form_start` nor `form_end` renders
           them. Omit this line and a rejected submit looks like a page that silently did nothing —
           on the one form in the context that sets a credential. #}
        {{ form_errors(newPasswordForm) }}

        {# The mismatch error and the defensive `WeakPassword` error are attached to the *repeated*
           field itself, not to either box, so they need rendering explicitly — rendering only the two
           children would swallow "The two passwords must match." entirely. #}
        {{ form_errors(newPasswordForm.plainPassword) }}
        {{ form_row(newPasswordForm.plainPassword.first) }}
        {{ form_row(newPasswordForm.plainPassword.second) }}

        <button type="submit">Set my new password</button>
    {{ form_end(newPasswordForm) }}

    {# Both boxes come back empty on a re-render (`PasswordType` defaults to `always_empty: true`), so
       a rejected password is not sitting in the HTML of a page a browser may cache. That matters here
       rather than in the abstract: this page *will* re-render on a weak password, with the same live
       link still stashed behind it (AC-23). #}

    <p><a href="{{ path('app_login') }}">Back to sign in</a></p>
{% endblock %}
